 "requested_at": "2026-11-12T19:52:04.283710Z",
 "text": "The clause is **enforceable**. It requires the tenant to do only what municipal law already requires of any dog or cat owner, and nothing in the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 conflicts with that.\n\nUnder the Act, a tenancy agreement term is void only where it conflicts with the Act or its regulations, for example by altering the rights, responsibilities or obligations the Act sets out. The clause does neither. It adds no new obligation beyond the municipal licensing and vaccination rules, and it does not reduce any protection the Act gives the tenant. The Act does not itself regulate pet licensing or vaccination, so the municipal bylaw supplies the requirement.\n\nTwo caveats:\n- The clause could not be enforced to bar a pet that is an assistance animal. The Human Rights Code overrides lease terms on that point, though it does not make this licensing requirement unenforceable.\n- I'm citing from memory and could not check the current consolidated text of the Act. Please confirm the section number against e-Laws (ontario.ca/laws) before relying on it.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
